) <tab:faults>

*Monte Carlo design.* A simulated unit draws every part within its tolerance: values from a normal distribution with a standard deviation of half the tolerance, truncated at the tolerance; electrolytic series resistance, transistor gain, tube amplification and emission and op-amp bandwidth from their own spreads, and the mains level within ±<< num(design.simulation.mains_tol_pct, 0) >>% as a common factor on both rectifier peak voltages. Units that reach a bench are old, so every second training, validation and calibration unit (<< design.simulation.aging.training_share_pct | round | int >>%) first drifts the way parts age: electrolytic capacitance ×<< design.simulation.aging.electrolytic_c[0] >>-<< design.simulation.aging.electrolytic_c[1] >> and series resistance ×<< design.simulation.aging.electrolytic_esr[0] >>-<< num(design.simulation.aging.electrolytic_esr[1], 0) >>, resistors ×<< num(design.simulation.aging.resistor[0], 2) >>-<< design.simulation.aging.resistor[1] >>, tube emission ×<< design.simulation.aging.triode_emission[0] >>-<< num(design.simulation.aging.triode_emission[1], 2) >> (ADR-039). A symptom is still judged against an as-new healthy unit. The aged pilot and test sets are aged throughout and have the same circuit mix as the as-new sets; units with every tolerance << design.simulation.tolerance_curve | join(', ') >> times wider are reported as a curve. Each hypothesis gets << design.simulation.train_draws >> training and << design.simulation.val_draws >> validation units. Every unit's random numbers derive from a seed built from the base seed, circuit, split, hypothesis and draw, so any unit can be regenerated alone; a test re-simulates stored units and compares them to six significant figures. Units are simulated in ngspice batches of << design.simulation.chunk_draws >>, with a three-step retry ladder (finer convergence settings, then a stiffer integration method) for batches that fail; failed units are kept with a reason and excluded from training. Of << num(m('library.simulations.total_draws'), 0) >> units, << m('library.simulations.failed_draws') >> failed and << m('library.simulations.retried_draws') >> needed a retry. A manifest records the hashes of every netlist, the device library and the tolerance code, and the simulator version. Each unit is simulated at its DC operating point, for gain at 20 Hz, 1 kHz and 20 kHz, for hum at 120 Hz, and for distortion at 1 kHz from a transient and Fourier analysis.

*Measurement model.* Every DC reading is simulated with the meter's << num(design.measurement.dmm_input_mohm, 0) >> MΩ input resistance across the node, one operating point per reading, as a real meter loads a high-impedance node << cite('fluke_11x_extended_specs') >>; the unloaded voltage is kept for the hazard audit and the hand checks. A simulated reading is that value plus instrument error: a meter of ±(<< num(design.measurement.dmm_pct, 1) >>% + << design.measurement.dmm_counts >> counts) on 6,000-count ranges, rounded to the range's resolution; an oscilloscope of ±<< num(design.measurement.scope_pct, 0) >>%, with floors for tiny signals; a distortion reading of ±<< num(design.measurement.thd_pct, 0) >>%. An unsoldered part tested out of circuit returns only "within" or "out of tolerance", with sensitivity << design.measurement.lift_sensitivity >> and << num(design.measurement.lift_false_positive_pct, 0) >>% false positives. The engine works in transformed units (DC voltages on a scale that is linear near zero and logarithmic above it, gains and hum in decibels), where instrument error is close to constant.

*Data splits.* @tab:splits lists every set. Evaluation sets sample symptomatic units uniformly: each case draws a hypothesis and a unit and keeps it only if the unit shows a symptom (up to << design.cases.max_attempts >> draws), so faults appear in proportion to how often they cause a symptom, which is not how often they fail in service; faults that never showed a symptom in training are left out.

