*Data quality.* Five checks guard the data. (1) Independent hand calculations reproduce every healthy bias point and gain (<< m('hand_calcs.rows_ok') >> of << m('hand_calcs.rows_checked') >> within tolerance). (2) Fault behavior is audited: << m('fault_audit.faults') >> faults drawn at random (up to two of each fault type per circuit) are re-solved without ngspice and without the netlist builder, by a solver written for the audit with the published device equations, and all << m('fault_audit.faults_agree') >> agree within twice the meter's accuracy (ADR-040; the first run's disagreements, all in the audit solver, are on record); an earlier spot check found the tube-model artifact, where a leakage resistor put an open-circuit cathode at half the plate voltage, fixed before any bar was set. (3) Stored units regenerate from their seeds, and the manifest ties every dataset to the code that produced it. (4) Complaint texts pass a leakage filter that rejects any text naming a part or a fault. Evaluation complaints come from a held-out paraphrase bank never used to write or tune the reader, with each shown symptom left out with probability << num(design.cases.complaint_omit_pct, 0) >>% and each other symptom added with << num(design.cases.complaint_add_pct, 0) >>%; a second bank, written by a separate author who never saw the rules and sealed until the lock, tests T18. (5) Ambiguity groups and symptom rates are reported per circuit, so a reader can see how often faults are indistinguishable or silent.

