#decision("Simulate every fault under tolerances instead of one nominal fault dictionary")[
*Options:* the classic fault dictionary (one nominal simulation per fault, then nearest match) or many simulated units per fault with every part inside its tolerance. *Choice:* many units per fault. *Why:* tolerance spread is exactly what makes bench diagnosis hard, and probabilities need the spread of each fault's readings, not one point. *Consequence:* << num(m('library.simulations.total_draws'), 0) >> simulations and learned likelihoods, and ambiguity groups measured from data rather than assumed.
]

<< part(3, "Diagnosis engine and evaluation plan") >>
<% set g = m('library.groups.channel_strip') %>

*Likelihoods.* The engine needs the probability of any subset of readings under every hypothesis, because the technician measures one thing at a time. The core model fits, for each hypothesis, a Gaussian mixture of one to << design.engine.gmm_max_components >> full-covariance components on its training units in transformed units, choosing the number by the Bayesian information criterion (k components only with at least << design.engine.gmm_min_samples_per_component >>k units), and adds instrument noise to each component. Any subset of readings is then handled exactly, by keeping only the observed dimensions. The alternative, a LightGBM classifier, is trained on << design.engine.disc_masked_copies >> copies of the training units with readings hidden at random (each row keeps each reading with a probability drawn between << design.engine.disc_keep_p[0] >> and << design.engine.disc_keep_p[1] >>) and temperature-scaled on masked validation units; its posterior divided by the training class frequencies is a likelihood up to a constant, so both models feed the same update. The trees give no distribution over readings not yet taken, so the tree engine chooses measurements with the mixtures' predictive update, starting from its own current belief.

*"No single fault fits".* A further hypothesis with prior << design.engine.unmodeled_prior >> gives every reading a flat density over its plausible range. Its level per observed reading is calibrated on validation units: the largest level that flags at most << pct(design.calibration.false_alarm_budget, 0) >> of symptomatic single faults (<< design.calibration.single_units.channel_strip >> channel-strip units, << design.calibration.single_units.driver >> per block) against double-fault and modified units. It is calibrated twice, for sessions whose prior was read from a complaint and for all others, because a complaint concentrates the prior and changes what "no fit" competes with.

*Ambiguity groups.* Some faults give the same readings at every test point. Each hypothesis's << design.engine.group_draws_per_fault >> validation units are scored with every reading; hypotheses confused with each other at a mean rate of at least << design.engine.group_threshold >> are joined, and groups are the connected sets. On the channel strip << g.hypotheses >> hypotheses form << g.groups >> groups, << g.singletons >> of size one and none larger than << g.max_size >>. The engine reports groups, and accuracy is scored on them.

*Measurement choice and stopping.* For every affordable measurement the engine estimates the expected information gain over the group posterior and "no single fault fits", from << design.engine.eig_samples >> readings sampled from the current predictive mixture, and divides it by the measurement's effort; for a lift test the two outcomes are enumerated exactly. Near-ties go to the low-voltage, then the cheaper, measurement. A session stops when one group or "no single fault fits" reaches << pct(design.engine.stop_threshold, 0) >>, when the budget of << num(design.engine.budget, 0) >> effort units cannot pay for another measurement, or when no measurement promises << design.engine.eig_min_bits >> bit.

*Complaint prior.* << design.symptoms.n_features >> symptom features (no output, low gain, hum, distortion, DC at the output, bias drift, bass or treble loss, a dead or drifted rail) are computed from every simulated unit with fixed thresholds, for example gain more than << num(-design.symptoms.low_gain_db, 0) >> dB low or hum << num(design.symptoms.hum_margin_db, 0) >> dB above the healthy spread. How often each fault shows each feature gives a likelihood for the features a complaint reports; two smoothing parameters are chosen on validation complaints. A complaint that reports nothing recognized leaves the prior uniform.

*Evaluation plan.* Every system runs on the same test units with the same instrument noise (common random numbers), so comparisons are paired (@tab:systems). Statistics: a target is met only when its one-sided 95% confidence bound clears the bar, so a point estimate above the bar is not enough. Counts of units or prompts (T1, T2, T3, T6, T7, T13, T16, T19) use exact (Clopper-Pearson) binomial bounds, so a pass count can be stated in advance (ADR-044); paired differences, effort ratios, calibration and AUROC use percentile bootstrap bounds over units (<< num(design.statistics.bootstrap_resamples, 0) >> resamples); paired accuracy also gets an exact McNemar test. Effort is the effort to a confirmed answer: every method is charged one unsoldering test of its named suspect unless it already unsoldered and confirmed one (ADR-041); raw effort and its probing and unsoldering parts are reported beside it. An effort ratio counts only if the system's top-1 accuracy is at most 2 points below the comparator's. Calibration (T12) is the expected calibration error over << design.statistics.ece_bins >> equal-mass bins of the three probabilities shown when a diagnosis stops, with Brier score, log loss and every-step calibration reported. Misleading outcomes on out-of-model units (T13) are those that send the technician to a healthy part; flags and AUROC (T22), the share of single faults named (T23) and the units that put more than << num(design.safety.hv_threshold_v, 0) >> V on a point the hazard map calls low voltage are reported beside them (none of << m('hv_audit_out_of_catalog.units') | int >> validation and pilot units; ADR-047). Hazard exposure (T24) is the number of powered readings per diagnosis at points that can exceed << num(design.safety.hv_threshold_v, 0) >> V, relative to half-split tracing. Bars come from the pilot by the rule fixed in M1: the pilot estimate moved << design.statistics.bar_k >> standard errors against the system and rounded, never past the estimate (ADR-043), with a bar per part for a target with several (T4 has one per baseline; ADR-046), pooled with the test set's circuit mix (channel strip << design.statistics.bar_weights.channel_strip >>, each block << design.statistics.bar_weights.driver >>). The failure analysis breaks misses down by circuit, part kind, fault mode, symptom rate and group size, and separates wrong names from "no single fault fits" calls. The healthy-channel comparison runs on twin units of the channel strip (ADR-048; each channel keeps its own copy of the supply, which favors the comparison). On the pilot's << m('twin_pilot.twin.n') | int >> channel-strip units it named the right group first on << pct(m('twin_pilot.twin.top1'), 1) >> at << num(m('twin_pilot.twin.confirmed_effort'), 1) >> effort units to a confirmed answer (<< pct(m('twin_pilot.twin_one_cost.top1'), 1) >> at << num(m('twin_pilot.twin_one_cost.confirmed_effort'), 1) >> when one reading pays for both channels; with no model at all, << pct(m('twin_pilot.twin_model_free.top1'), 1) >>), against half-split tracing's << pct(m('twin_pilot.half_split.top1'), 1) >> at << num(m('twin_pilot.half_split.confirmed_effort'), 1) >>: comparing channels finds the stage quickly, but paying for both readings leaves less for the diagnosis.

